1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,230
On the Moon and on Mars, there
are machines NASA left behind.

2
00:00:04,363 --> 00:00:06,432
A lander from 1967.

3
00:00:06,607 --> 00:00:08,439
Rovers parked by astronauts.

4
00:00:08,479 --> 00:00:11,172
A little helicopter that flew 72 times.

5
00:00:11,346 --> 00:00:15,664
They're abandoned, but not forgotten:
each one still tells us what those worlds

6
00:00:15,704 --> 00:00:17,903
do to the things we build.

7
00:00:18,303 --> 00:00:20,984
When Apollo 12 landed next to Surveyor 3,

8
00:00:21,024 --> 00:00:23,140
the crew brought pieces of it home.

9
00:00:23,331 --> 00:00:27,781
After 31 months on the Moon, they
were darkened by dust and sunlight,

10
00:00:27,884 --> 00:00:30,035
but nothing had seriously failed.

11
00:00:30,227 --> 00:00:34,659
Later, Apollo 17's rover lost a fender,
and the astronauts fixed it with maps

12
00:00:34,699 --> 00:00:35,519
and tape.

13
00:00:35,710 --> 00:00:38,350
Up there, there are no spare parts.

14
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:42,110
On the Moon, a broken wrench
is not a small problem.

15
00:00:42,285 --> 00:00:48,182
The next one is 384,000 kilometres away,
and sending it can cost up to a million

16
00:00:48,222 --> 00:00:50,366
dollars per kilogram.

17
00:00:50,766 --> 00:00:56,247
In 2026, NASA Glenn grew a plastic inside
bacteria and mixed it with simulated

18
00:00:56,287 --> 00:01:00,315
Moon and Mars dust: a material
that could be made on site.

19
00:01:00,506 --> 00:01:01,632
But which mix?

20
00:01:01,823 --> 00:01:04,964
How much dust, and is it
strong enough for a wrench?

21
00:01:05,155 --> 00:01:07,897
You can't test every combination on Earth.

22
00:01:08,297 --> 00:01:11,069
So, for the Abandoned but
not Forgotten challenge,

23
00:01:11,109 --> 00:01:13,441
we built the Moon-Material Simulator.

24
00:01:13,632 --> 00:01:16,694
Pick a binder, a soil,
and how the part is made.

25
00:01:16,885 --> 00:01:21,057
Instantly, a physics model corrected by
machine learning predicts eight properties,

26
00:01:21,097 --> 00:01:24,847
shows how sure it is, and tells you
whether each number is backed by real lab

27
00:01:24,887 --> 00:01:25,650
data.

28
00:01:25,841 --> 00:01:29,608
It runs in any browser,
and it's open source.

29
00:01:30,008 --> 00:01:34,910
Our science stands on NASA's own
data: Glenn's bioplastic reports,

30
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,881
Kennedy's regolith-printing
studies, a 2003 NASA report,

31
00:01:38,921 --> 00:01:40,814
and peer-reviewed papers.

32
00:01:40,988 --> 00:01:46,046
We transcribed 1,470 measured
values, each tied to its page.

33
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:50,107
Then we built an auditor that finds
every number again in the original PDF.

34
00:01:50,282 --> 00:01:51,814
It never edits data.

35
00:01:51,988 --> 00:01:57,550
Zero transcription errors, and 181
problems found in the papers themselves.

36
00:01:57,962 --> 00:02:03,150
Physics alone isn't enough: its
strength error averaged 230 percent.

37
00:02:03,324 --> 00:02:06,779
So we kept the physics and taught
a Gaussian process its mistakes,

38
00:02:06,819 --> 00:02:09,292
using 152 real measurements.

39
00:02:09,467 --> 00:02:14,591
On mixes it never saw, that error
drops from 230 to 34 percent,

40
00:02:14,670 --> 00:02:19,170
stiffness from 65 to 19,
and density from 16 to 5.

41
00:02:19,344 --> 00:02:22,354
A completely new study
is harder, and we say so.

42
00:02:22,477 --> 00:02:24,556
And every prediction
links to its evidence:

43
00:02:24,678 --> 00:02:30,087
we predict 117 megapascals,
and the paper measured 119.

44
00:02:30,487 --> 00:02:34,925
Back to our wrench: NASA's bioplastic
with 38 percent lunar dust.

45
00:02:35,116 --> 00:02:39,658
It breaks at 71 newtons, just short
of the 72 needed to tighten a bolt.

46
00:02:39,849 --> 00:02:42,603
At lunar noon it softens
to a sixth of that.

47
00:02:42,794 --> 00:02:44,825
At lunar night it's
almost twice as strong,

48
00:02:44,865 --> 00:02:48,131
but brittle: it snaps instead of bending.

49
00:02:48,531 --> 00:02:50,227
So we ask the optimizer.

50
00:02:50,402 --> 00:02:55,383
It tests nearly five thousand mixes and
finds one made entirely from local resources:

51
00:02:55,516 --> 00:02:59,293
45 percent dust and 15
percent basalt fibre.

52
00:02:59,468 --> 00:03:03,945
Safety factor 1.7, and it
bends before it breaks.

53
00:03:04,345 --> 00:03:08,590
Ask it for a rocket nozzle, and it
honestly says no plastic survives.

54
00:03:08,781 --> 00:03:13,191
But a kit of 25 small parts printed on
site keeps 10 kilograms off the launch

55
00:03:13,231 --> 00:03:16,771
pad: up to 10 million dollars.

56
00:03:17,171 --> 00:03:21,175
We're Dreams of X: five friends, one team.

57
00:03:21,349 --> 00:03:26,435
Mahdin Islam Mukim, Maliha
Sanjana, Lamisa Yeasmin Nakia,

58
00:03:26,475 --> 00:03:29,102
Yousuf Abdullah and Habibullah Sharif.

59
00:03:29,277 --> 00:03:33,141
We researched, designed,
built and tested it together.

60
00:03:33,541 --> 00:03:37,023
The machines we left behind taught
us what the Moon does to materials.

61
00:03:37,215 --> 00:03:40,794
Now our simulator helps engineers choose
the right mix before a single sample

62
00:03:40,834 --> 00:03:41,711
is printed.

63
00:03:41,902 --> 00:03:44,693
And it's for learning,
too: students, teachers,

64
00:03:44,733 --> 00:03:48,757
and anyone who loves rockets and space
can explore NASA's real data and see the

65
00:03:48,797 --> 00:03:50,709
measurement behind every answer.

66
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:52,807
The same method works for Mars.

67
00:03:52,847 --> 00:03:54,189
Try it yourself.
